PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.5/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: This is a strong, unusually coherent set. Nearly every level has one readable idea, a named trap, and a "forecast shows it in week 1" fairness rule. That makes it a better teacher than most of the genre. The late-game casual win rates average about 63%, which is on target. Weaknesses:
- The "leave something unbuilt / decoy useless station" aha is overused (L8, 17, 32, 46, 89, 94, 107, 117, 120).
- Several levels re-teach the same insight in a new setting. L22, L62 and L71 repeat L5/L11/L35, L70 repeats L9, L94 repeats L86/L89, and L41 repeats L39.
- Boards stay small (≤10 stops, ≤3 lines), so the endgame is harder through numbers rather than new thinking.
- Many bosses and the finale list their trap rate as "?" (search cap), so the "no hidden fatal move" claim is not proven where it matters most.
- The heritage "never full, even once" rule sits awkwardly with the calm "grace window" pillar. L109 and L111 lose by week 3 with no grace. Curve: The pacing is good. Cities teach one mechanic alone (tunnels L13, carriages L25, hills L37, ferries L49, snow L61, interchanges L73, loops L85, waves L97, heritage L109). Each is then practised, twisted, combined, run as a boss, followed by a breather and a mastery send-off. Breathers sit after bosses (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115). Casual win rates fall from ~88% to ~45-60%, and there is no run of more than two easy levels. Problems:
1. Interchanges (L73), market day (L74) and heritage (L109) arrive very late, even though the doc's core loop offers interchange cards from the start. Teach a cut-down version in the first 20 levels.
2. The "leave it unbuilt / pretty decoy" trick is reused about nine times. By L107 and L120 experienced players spot it instantly.
3. Many levels reuse the same trap shapes ("one long snake loses, split it", "heavy pair shares a line", "stadium opens late, line ends there"). From L60 on, the twists are mostly parameter changes to earlier insights, not new insight.
4. The L90, L100, L102 and L118 casual rates (44-49%) sit next to 90%+ breathers. This is acceptable but spiky. A mid-difficulty buffer would help.
5. The last quarter has no board bigger than 9 stops. A large sandbox-style finale board would suit the "watch it flow" fantasy.
6. Levels lost in the last week (wk6-8) rely on the "restart from earlier week" feature. Make sure this is shown prominently.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L22 (6/10): This is a disguise of L5, L9 and L11 (heavy stop should be a line end). The new tunnel layer adds little. The board is 7 stops in two tidy columns and the solution is symmetric. → Break the symmetry: give H uneven demand per bank, or make one tunnel unavailable for the second line, so the player has to choose a route and not copy it.
L41 (6/10): This is a disguise of L39 (hill vs long way round). Skilled 89% and casual 75% on a single-line, 6-stop board is not a new idea, and the empty C leg is wasted space. → Replace with a two-line hill level where a hill is on the only route to one stop (so avoiding it is impossible) and the question is which line carries it.
L62 (6/10): This is the same insight as L5 (make the heavy stop a line end). Casual 73% vs skilled 77% shows skill barely helps, so players are guessing among four equal-looking openings. → Replace with a snow-specific lesson (for example, a heavy stop whose two plausible fixes differ only in the snow weeks), or tune the board so skilled reaches 90%+ by making the forecast reveal the mill's overload on week 1.
L70 (6/10): This is a disguise of L9 (heavy corner pair gets its own line). A 2x3 grid is the same shape, and casual 56% on a board with seven openings makes it feel like a guess. → Add a differently-weighted second pair so the crossing choice has a real trade-off, or remove and put in a snow-specific interchange lesson.
L71 (6/10): This is the same shape as L35 (late ground, both-or-one line on). It even admits the earlier answer 'scrapes a win'. Casual 61% / skilled 83% with an unproven trap rate for a boss is rough. → Change the geometry: make H reachable from only one arm, so the lopsided-crowd choice is a different decision, and run a trap-rate sweep.
L94 (6/10): This is a disguise of L86/L89 (close the loop, skip a quiet stop). Casual 55% for a trivial K spur choice is harsh and the lesson has already been taught twice. → Cut it, or turn it into a budget puzzle with two candidate stops to drop where only one choice closes the loop.
L7 (7/10): Only 5 weeks and 9 stops with 2 lines in hand is almost no decision. The 3rd-star puzzle (end on the boathouse) is a one-off trick, so the level is forgettable. → Add a second, optional boathouse-style dead end so the 1-line puzzle needs a real trace. Alternatively give it a gentle visual theme and keep it short.
L8 (7/10): The aha 'skip B' depends on B being a Star destination while F, also a Star, opens later. This is confusing to explain, and the Star glyph logic is muddy. Casual 83% for a combine with a single line reads as guessy. → Make B a clearly named useless stop (like L17/L32) or give F/H different glyphs so the reason to skip B is legible in the route preview.
L11 (7/10): At 8 stops it is barely bigger than L6, and the boss insight (end a line at G) repeats L4/L6. Search-capped trap rate '?' means the fairness is unproven. The safety-net card makes the boss forgiving, which feels low-stakes. → Add a second decision (for example, make the I demand uneven across two exits), or reduce the card's rescue power so the week-1 plan matters. Run a full trap-rate sweep.
L12 (7/10): The 'tidy' near-trap still wins, so the transfer aha is only worth the 3rd star. Casual 89% is near-breather territory for a mastery, and it follows the L11 boss. → Raise the goal so only the shared-transfer plan clears it, or keep as is and swap the order with a breather.
L16 (7/10): The tunnel card is a decoy whose failure is only visible in the F ring forecast. The lesson 'read demand cards' is fine, but the board has three rules in one and the correct pick is obvious once you check F. It is a quiz, not a puzzle. → Make the E quay a larger rider source (about 4) so the tunnel card is a real temptation, and make +2 tracks leave a visible cost.
L25 (7/10): The carriage appears only at L25, so the core loop's train/carriage cards are absent for the first quarter of the game. The board is one forced line with one move: add the carriage. No aha. → Add a second option such as a short split that also works at the cost of a transfer, so the carriage is a choice and not a mandatory button.
L31 (7/10): Casual 92% is right, but the board is a plain diamond plus a stem with no distinctive texture, and 'use one line' is a fair repeat of L7's 3rd star. → Give a themed goal (for example 'fly the kite in one line and finish the loop-free tail') or add a fun route-preview moment so it feels like a gift.
L36 (7/10): +2 tracks is a dominated option, so there is no real card decision. One line, 6 stops: the whole level is 'draw the snake, take the carriage'. → Make +2 tracks tempting (for example an unconnected stop that opens wk3) so the card choice is a real read.
L42 (7/10): The trap is the carriage on the hill, which mirrors L40 reversed. The 'read the load meter' lesson is good, but both cards win if put on the lane, so the real decision is only 'which line', and week-1 play reveals it. Boss feels like a puzzle, not a test of everything. → Make the two cards mutually different in cost (line costs a track, carriage costs seats elsewhere) and add a late station so the player must also plan ahead.
L48 (7/10): Casual 72% for a 'gentle send-off', and the +1 line card is a clear decoy once you see the transfer. Three lines with a crowded hill make the board busy. → Reduce goal slightly and drop the extra starting line to simplify, or let the +1 line also be a viable but costly answer.
L53 (7/10): This is the same lesson as L28/L34 (carriage goes where the bottleneck is), and casual 63% / skilled 89% is notably harder than its neighbours without a new idea. The ferry doubling is a mechanic gloss, not an aha. → Give the player a second plausible bottleneck (for example a 12-rider spur on the land line) so the carriage choice needs a rider count.
L55 (7/10): It reuses L19's template exactly and, with the optional ferry, the ring has little to decide. 8 stops with 7 tracks is generous to the point of dull. → Add a scenic constraint (one stop that pays off a 3rd star only if on the ferry) so there is one small thing to find.
L60 (7/10): The optional island G is the 3rd star, but with 'deliver every rider' as the star the level is more arithmetic than aha. It follows a L59 boss at 58% casual with a 79% gentle send-off, which is OK. → Make G's demand surprising (3 riders needing the other boat) so the player must check the cards.
L64 (7/10): Casual 61% with 3 lines on a 5-stop board is a sharp rise for what is a re-run of L49/L51 with a halved boat. The lesson 'keep island riders off the boat' is the third time it has been taught. → Swap in a snow week that makes the ferry unusable so the answer becomes a tunnel/loop alternative, or just drop it for a unique layout.
L67 (7/10): The text says it earns the 'second star' while the star line says 'no amber', so intent and star criteria are inconsistent. Also near-identical to L7 in structure. → Align the star text. Make the long north walk visually obvious (longer drawn edge) so the aha is a 'ooh' and not a count.
L72 (7/10): Both cards win, so it is gentle but low-stakes. The 3rd star (2 tracks spare) is quite hidden. → Show the spare-track star goal in the card preview so players can weigh the two cards.
L73 (7/10): Interchange is a core-loop card item but is only taught at L73. The lesson (a stop that waits) is real, but it overlaps with the 'late track' idea from L4. → Move a cut-down interchange tutorial earlier, around L20-30, and keep this as the practise.
L74 (7/10): The lesson reduces to 'split into two lines', a repeat of L3. The market spike is only a number bump. → Have the market stop sit on the busiest line so the trade-off between line length and spike is real.
L76 (7/10): Clever, but the aha (nearest-glyph redirect) is only discoverable through the route preview and a 0-rider station. Skilled 81% and casual 56% suggest many players cannot find it. → Add a pre-market forecast hint on F (ring amber with a pulse on G) and make G's glyph label highlighted when placed.

level-design score 7.5/10 — This is a strong, unusually coherent set. Nearly every level has one readable idea, a named trap, and a "forecast shows it in week 1" fairness rule. That makes it a better teacher than most of the genre. The late-game casual win rates average about 63%, which is on target. Weaknesses:
- The "leave something unbuilt / decoy useless station" aha is overused (L8, 17, 32, 46, 89, 94, 107, 117, 120).
- Several levels re-teach the same insight in a new setting. L22, L62 and L71 repeat L5/L11/L35, L70 repeats L9, L94 repeats L86/L89, and L41 repeats L39.
- Boards stay small (≤10 stops, ≤3 lines), so the endgame is harder through numbers rather than new thinking.
- Many bosses and the finale list their trap rate as "?" (search cap), so the "no hidden fatal move" claim is not proven where it matters most.
- The heritage "never full, even once" rule sits awkwardly with the calm "grace window" pillar. L109 and L111 lose by week 3 with no grace.
curve: The pacing is good. Cities teach one mechanic alone (tunnels L13, carriages L25, hills L37, ferries L49, snow L61, interchanges L73, loops L85, waves L97, heritage L109). Each is then practised, twisted, combined, run as a boss, followed by a breather and a mastery send-off. Breathers sit after bosses (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115). Casual win rates fall from ~88% to ~45-60%, and there is no run of more than two easy levels. Problems:
1. Interchanges (L73), market day (L74) and heritage (L109) arrive very late, even though the doc's core loop offers interchange cards from the start. Teach a cut-down version in the first 20 levels.
2. The "leave it unbuilt / pretty decoy" trick is reused about nine times. By L107 and L120 experienced players spot it instantly.
3. Many levels reuse the same trap shapes ("one long snake loses, split it", "heavy pair shares a line", "stadium opens late, line ends there"). From L60 on, the twists are mostly parameter changes to earlier insights, not new insight.
4. The L90, L100, L102 and L118 casual rates (44-49%) sit next to 90%+ breathers. This is acceptable but spiky. A mid-difficulty buffer would help.
5. The last quarter has no board bigger than 9 stops. A large sandbox-style finale board would suit the "watch it flow" fantasy.
6. Levels lost in the last week (wk6-8) rely on the "restart from earlier week" feature. Make sure this is shown prominently.
